                    {
                        "translation_id": "f5324823-299f-4c86-87e1-878da5210fce",
                        "source_entry_id": "e3439804-f75c-3193-be6e-0bb3696ccb77",
                        "source_sense_id": "0a1eff48-c3da-35ba-86ef-49668aeb5df8",
                        "target_lang_word": "vagueness",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "vagueness",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e12554a5-4071-3ebd-89e1-c320ea67b0f6",
                                        "entry_id": "8f1b7eb5-7957-37da-ae9d-0c6b134be810",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The condition of being imprecise or unclear in meaning: vague."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2306596c-c1ff-4cdf-b7bb-2ab489593b35",
                                                "sense_id": "e12554a5-4071-3ebd-89e1-c320ea67b0f6",
                                                "text": "Near-synonyms: ambiguousness, ambiguity"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f7f309ca-8299-46a4-ad8b-360b4a1fbe80",
                                                "sense_id": "e12554a5-4071-3ebd-89e1-c320ea67b0f6",
                                                "text": "On Wednesday night, a federal judge issued a preliminary injunction against a new California law that makes physicians subject to professional discipline for sharing COVID-19 \"misinformation\" with their patients. U.S. District Judge William B. Shubb concluded that California's definition of misinformation is unconstitutionally vague, failing to give doctors fair notice of which conduct the statute covers, which is a basic requirement of due process. That vagueness is especially problematic in this context, Shubb said, because it is apt to have a chilling effect on speech protected by the First Amendment."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "077555c1-2c08-386b-a353-e07908c6618a",
                                        "entry_id": "8f1b7eb5-7957-37da-ae9d-0c6b134be810",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Something which is vague, or an instance or example of vagueness."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3c22c6b4-f5a4-4fd4-b99b-1d0098207232",
                                                "sense_id": "077555c1-2c08-386b-a353-e07908c6618a",
                                                "text": "If a man's deep and conscientious regard for the truth be such that he cannot, consistently with the requisitions of his moral nature, repeat to others mere vaguenesses and uncertainties, he will naturally give such strict and serious attention to the present objects of inquiry and knowledge, that they will remain in his memory afterward with remarkable distinctness and permanency."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "368d02a8-ae18-4495-b5d4-121221c17316",
                        "source_entry_id": "e3439804-f75c-3193-be6e-0bb3696ccb77",
                        "source_sense_id": "0a1eff48-c3da-35ba-86ef-49668aeb5df8",
                        "target_lang_word": "dimness",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "dimness",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "da0bbf52-7e60-3931-8813-9170dda4899c",
                                        "entry_id": "79be4cf6-d04e-3741-aa0e-dc4172febfbb",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The state of being dim, poorly illuminated, almost dark."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3e618bb3-1d1d-43be-af03-f104a49f480a",
                                                "sense_id": "da0bbf52-7e60-3931-8813-9170dda4899c",
                                                "text": "So likewise a day comes when the Runic Thor, with his Eddas, must withdraw into dimness; and many an African Mumbo-Jumbo and Indian Pawpaw be utterly abolished."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "1872cc43-a726-41e0-844d-320f3edd94f9",
                        "source_entry_id": "e3439804-f75c-3193-be6e-0bb3696ccb77",
                        "source_sense_id": "0a1eff48-c3da-35ba-86ef-49668aeb5df8",
                        "target_lang_word": "faintness",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "faintness",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c7dc2b6f-5400-3f55-a95b-ac704acc594d",
                                        "entry_id": "19fac1ab-e1f6-310a-9ca5-692958ee0ac2",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The property of being or feeling faint."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a91adafd-68ed-467f-ac0d-dde616c7fa10",
                                                "sense_id": "c7dc2b6f-5400-3f55-a95b-ac704acc594d",
                                                "text": "And he first took exceptions at this badge, / Pronouncing that the paleness of this flower / Bewray'd the faintness of my master's heart."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "09186693-88c0-4811-b4e7-236a05cf62a5",
                                                "sense_id": "c7dc2b6f-5400-3f55-a95b-ac704acc594d",
                                                "text": "The confusion, in which impressions are sometimes involved, proceeds only from their faintness and unsteadiness, not from any capacity in the mind to receive any impression, which in its real existence has no particular degree nor proportion."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1d66ce8c-a7a6-45cc-a0f3-5335ed065c4b",
                                                "sense_id": "c7dc2b6f-5400-3f55-a95b-ac704acc594d",
                                                "text": "The faintness of the voice was pitiable and dreadful. It was not the faintness of physical weakness, though confinement and hard fare no doubt had their part in it. Its deplorable peculiarity was, that it was the faintness of solitude and disuse. It was like the last feeble echo of a sound made long and long ago."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b1314eee-3240-46b6-b9ce-128e18bc3cf9",
                                                "sense_id": "c7dc2b6f-5400-3f55-a95b-ac704acc594d",
                                                "text": "The humming sound and the unvarying white light induced a sort of faintness, an empty feeling inside his head."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
